RC, RL and RLC transients and energy (@em-transients: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @electromagnetism, #source "../sources/University-Physics-Volume-2.lingua#r_5JQ0CPQ0RNJNVH175Y9V305J5Q", #source "../sources/Electricity-and-Magnetism-MIT-8-02T.lingua#r_00CWY37VDJSBRF5D6A8BCD7SXT", #electromagnetism "../../../../inquiry/metrology/applications/Time-and-frequency.lingua#r_49ST8P3R51TD437BJC5H0WH9TC") { r_4F9B677SF6134C9JSNNYZ94QCJ
**Read:** [University Physics Volume 2 — OpenStax](../sources/University-Physics-Volume-2.lingua#r_5JQ0CPQ0RNJNVH175Y9V305J5Q), RC circuits and chapter 14 inductance; [Electricity and Magnetism — MIT 8.02T and TEAL notes](../sources/Electricity-and-Magnetism-MIT-8-02T.lingua#r_00CWY37VDJSBRF5D6A8BCD7SXT), complete transient and inductance notes.

A resistor dissipates energy, a capacitor stores electric-field energy and an inductor stores magnetic-field energy within the linear ideal model. Their state cannot change arbitrarily fast without the corresponding impulsive current or voltage. Derive the differential equation and initial condition rather than selecting a remembered exponential by appearance.

For an initially uncharged C connected through R to a constant source V₀, V_C = V₀(1−exp(−t/RC)). At t = RC the fraction is 1−e⁻¹, approximately 0.63212. The source ultimately supplies CV₀², half stored and half dissipated in the ideal R, independent of how small a positive R is. The R→0 transient is singular; an actual arrangement introduces inductance, radiation and finite source response.

Derive RL growth/decay and the damped RLC oscillator, separating underdamped, critical and overdamped regimes. Track stored energy, dissipation and source work throughout the transient. Real inductors have winding resistance, core loss and parasitic capacitance; real capacitors have loss and leakage. A circuit equation is credible only over the band in which its element models remain adequate.

